clean-arch/step2-edge-effect-2026-11-10/council/sender-review-codex.log:2989:3. **Control and separation — not serious with stated limits.** The control compares 239 canonical requests and saved answers, listed ledger tables and adapter counts. It excludes `judgements.call_id`, does not compare SQLite’s FTS tables or `sqlite_sequence`, and compares `judge-requests.json` only by day, sequence, view and shown IDs. The archive comparison uses request hashes and answers, not historical wire bytes or full response metadata. Thus “every table” and “judge requests equal” are broader than the check. Sources: [edge_replay.py](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step2-edge-effect-2026-11-10/edge_replay.py:39), [edge_replay.py](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step2-edge-effect-2026-11-10/edge_replay.py:134).
clean-arch/step2-edge-effect-2026-11-10/council/sender-review-codex.log:2999:6. **Census — not serious for the stated final count.** The saved ledger has 700 links; the five listed have neither a direct Step 1 link nor a later link in the census, and each has an eligible older linked record. I checked the saved revision and link rows for all three affected records: none of the five has an additional later revision relation that would also have carried that same link. The count of five therefore holds for these records. **Limitation:** the census’s `old link id < new link id` test would miss an alternative relation whose old link was written later than the new link, even if that relation was processed later and would have carried it. The method should not be assumed correct for arbitrary data. Sources: [inheritance_census.py](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step2-edge-effect-2026-11-10/inheritance_census.py:57), [adapter.py](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-2026-11-09/adapter.py:209), [saved census](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-step2-edge-effect-2026-11-10/inheritance-census.json).
